WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.980
How does a stolen login
become weeks of downtime?

00:00:03.535 --> 00:00:05.305
Ransomware takes four steps.

00:00:06.717 --> 00:00:09.167
First, someone walks in
through the front door.

00:00:09.987 --> 00:00:13.717
A VPN with no second factor,
or an unpatched edge device.

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:17.207
They sign in like an employee,
so no alarm fires.

00:00:17.657 --> 00:00:22.127
Consider a second factor that resists
phishing, and a small, patched edge.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.040
Second, one laptop
becomes the whole company.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:28.900
On a flat network with
shared admin accounts,

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.240
the attacker reaches domain admin.

00:00:31.575 --> 00:00:33.365
Then everything is reachable.

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:35.100
Consider segmentation,

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.500
tiered admin and endpoint detection.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.640
Third, they copy everything first.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.080
The night before,
they copy the data and encrypt later.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:47.900
A leak site threatens to
publish the data unless we pay.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.690
That makes it a data breach,
not just downtime.

00:00:51.275 --> 00:00:54.865
Consider egress monitoring and
least-privilege data access.

00:00:56.017 --> 00:00:57.767
Fourth, the backups go first.

00:00:58.122 --> 00:01:01.872
At two on a Friday night,
the attacker deletes the online backup.

00:01:02.227 --> 00:01:03.837
Then the ransom is demanded.

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:08.307
Consider an immutable offline copy,
and a tested full restore.

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.660
What good looks like.

00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:12.715
Two numbers decide everything.

00:01:13.075 --> 00:01:16.585
How many days can we be down,
and how much data can we lose?

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.120
Every control is priced against them.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.100
Decide now, in calm,
whether we would ever pay.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.160
Paying a sanctioned group
can itself be unlawful.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:30.280
Report to the police either way, and
check No More Ransom for a free decryptor.

00:01:32.063 --> 00:01:33.273
A suggested first step.

00:01:33.558 --> 00:01:35.488
Ask the CIO for four numbers.

00:01:35.848 --> 00:01:40.398
External logins without a second factor,
and days to patch a critical flaw.

00:01:40.773 --> 00:01:45.263
The date of the last full restore test,
and the share of endpoints with detection.

00:01:47.017 --> 00:01:48.707
Four steps to a standstill.

00:01:48.922 --> 00:01:51.092
Break the chain, and test the restore.

